We present new findings regarding the upper bounds on the effective electron-neutrino mass ($m_{
u_e}$) derived from the KATRIN experiment. These findings are based on reanalyzing the initial 1,000 inverse-KATRIN days of data, which were collected between May 2019 and May 2020. The analysis focuses on systematic uncertainties related to the beta-spectrum shape, specifically those arising from the energy calibration and the background model. The results indicate that the upper bounds on the $m_{
u_e}$ mass are less than 1.55 eV/c$^2$ at a 90% confidence level, considering both the background and energy scale. The results also show that the background and energy scale uncertainties contribute 0.003 eV/c$^2$ and 0.004 eV/c$^2$, respectively, to the upper bounds on the $m_{
